<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">comsigrev</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">COMSIG Rev</journal-id><journal-title>COMSIG review</journal-title><publisher><publisher-name>Chiropractors &#x00026; Osteopaths Musculo-Skeletal Interest Group</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2050372</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>AN INTRODUCTION TO PROBABILITY DISTRIBUTIONS</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Ugoni</surname><given-names>Antony</given-names></name><degrees>B.Sc.(Hons.).</degrees></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Walker</surname><given-names>Bruce F.</given-names></name><degrees>D.C.</degrees></contrib></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>1</day><month>3</month><year>1995</year></pub-date><volume>4</volume><issue>1</issue><fpage>16</fpage><lpage>23</lpage><copyright-statement>Chiropractic &#x00026; Osteopathic College of Australasia</copyright-statement><copyright-year>1995</copyright-year><abstract><p>Probability allows us to infer from a sample to a population. In fact, inference is a tool of probability theory. This paper looks briefly at the Binomial, Poisson, and Normal distributions. These are probability distributions, which are used extensively in inference. An understanding of these distributions will assist the chiropractor and osteopath to critically appraise the literature.	  </p></abstract><kwd-group><kwd>Probability distributions</kwd><kwd>biostatistics</kwd><kwd>chiropractic</kwd><kwd>osteopathy</kwd></kwd-group></article-meta></front></article>